// @emit-error annotate the return type
// THE REP CLIFF, pinned at the one cell it still bites, and it is LOUD here where it was a
// SILENT WRONG VALUE before.
//
// `function describe(s: S) { return s.kind }` returns the tag with no annotation. When the
// arms' tag fields are BARE literals the read is a plain string and the function runs
// unannotated (`inferred-tag-return.vl`). When they are NAMED litunion aliases the same read
// yields the interned i32 ATOM — and the join TYPE is `"circle" | "rect"` in BOTH cases, so
// nothing downstream can tell them apart. The function returned an id and `print` at the call
// site did not widen it: `0` where `circle` was expected, `vl check` rc 0, valid module.
//
// WHY IT IS REFUSED RATHER THAN WIDENED. The widen exists (`emitAtomToStr`) and the return
// site can see the value is an atom. What it cannot do is make the SIGNATURE agree: the
// result kind is classified in another pass, from the same expression, and the two would have
// to reach the same answer independently. There is no recorded type to agree through — the
// A20 inferred-return row for such a function is EMPTY, measured: `inferRetRowOf` and
// `inferRetRowByNode` both answer -1 for it. Widening on one side only is how the invalid
// wasm in the sibling fixture happened.
//
// Two routes lift this, both bigger than a reject: give un-named literal types the interned
// ATOM rep (A5c, owner-ruled 2026-08-16, whose own note names extending that rep as its
// prerequisite), or record the inferred return type where both passes can read it.
//
// THE REMEDY IS ONE WORD and the message says so: `: string` compiles and runs for BOTH
// spellings today, because a `string` return already widens an atom.
type KC = "circle"
type KR = "rect"
type C = { kind: KC, r: f64 }
type R = { kind: KR, w: f64 }
type S = C | R

function describe(s: S) { return s.kind }
print(describe({ kind: "circle", r: 1.0 }))
